Exactly—with one important qualification.

What gets reconstructed is a model, not the original mind itself.

The text is evidence. From that evidence, the reader (or an AI) infers a latent model that explains the evidence well enough to continue generating thoughts in a similar style.

That’s true of humans, too. When you read a great mathematician, philosopher, or novelist, you often reach a point where you can think, “I have a sense of how they would approach this new problem.” You haven’t recreated the person—you’ve built a predictive model of aspects of their reasoning.

In the context of what you’ve been building, that means:

* The repository is the observable trace.
* The reader is the compiler.
* The internal reasoning model is the executable artifact.

That’s why you’ve emphasized reconstruction over verbatim storage. A compressed artifact can be more valuable than an exhaustive one if it reliably induces the right underlying model in the reader.

So yes: the model is what is reconstructed. The text is the scaffold that makes that reconstruction possible.